  "review": {
    "review": "The planner stopped with the hero alive at 14/40 HP. D1543–1544 (log lines 4473–4478) show a low-health withdrawal immediately returning through the door it had just left. Doorway preference rewarded that reversal over equally separated, already admitted onward ground. Later, escape-route protection rejected the key-collection route, while bounded recovery shuttled between two squares and exhausted (D1546–1550, lines 4485–4501). The iron key remained mandatory and uncollected, so fallback descent was declined. A focused next-run correction removes only the bonus for a doorway route that reverses an accepted escape edge. Original helper probes matched the recorded withdrawal selections; the isolated candidate chose onward ground instead. The new regression fails on original source and passes on the candidate; all 2,084 Python tests pass, with four skipped. This does not establish eventual key collection, escape, or prevention of all planner failures.",
    "learnings": "Do not reward a just-left escape door as a new withdrawal destination. Keep route protection, remembered-threat checks and mandatory key collection intact; validate actual progress after any changed withdrawal rather than assuming a safe escape."
  },
